    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Background: The HIV epidemic continues to threaten the public health of the United States with an estimated 1.2 million people living with HIV despite the development of antiretroviral therapy (ART). Wastewater-based Epidemiology (WBE) offers a novel method to build a population-wide surveillance system for infectious diseases without relying on individual data. We were interested in using WBE to help end the HIV epidemic by using wastewater to figure out where public health authorities should target screening efforts. We preliminarily detected HIV virus in two sewersheds in Santa Clara County and San Francisco County, and are now conducting further studies regarding how to interpret those results. We are conducting a study to measure how community HIV levels are portrayed in wastewater quantification. The aims of the SWAN (Stanford Waste Analysis Network) study are (1) to develop and test the WBE quantification methods on artificially infected samples and (2) to apply these methods in samples from viremic participants with HIV to quantify how much viremic participants shed into wastewater.\r\nMethods: For Aim 1, stool and urine samples from healthy subjects alongside wastewater samples from local wastewater facilities were inoculated with HIV-1. The HIV-1 RNA levels were measured using ddPCR and RT-ddPCR. For Aim 2, stool, urine, and blood samples were collected from participants with HIV recruited from clinics in San Mateo and Santa Clara counties. The HIV-1 RNA levels in urine and stool were measured in these samples using similar extraction and quantification techniques as used in Aim 1.\r\nResults: Aim 1 identified the limit of detection for our wastewater quantification method to be approximately at 60,000 copies/mL of HIV-1. The HIV-1 RNA was detected with this technique in all inoculated samples with the wastewater types showing the highest recovery rates. In Aim\r\n2, we found that HIV-1 RNA was detected in the stool and concentrated urine but not unconcentrated urine of participants with HIV.\r\nConclusion: Our study develops wastewater surveillance for HIV by verifying the method’s performance in standards, inoculated samples, and by quantifying how much viremic participants shed into urine and stool. Future work and completion of the SWAN study will provide a robust foundation to develop a wastewater surveillance system for HIV.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
